14:50:28 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re right to be skeptical. “HD Voice” often improves the *person you eventually speak to*, not the hold music. Hold music is commonly generated by a separate part of a company’s phone system, and that path is frequently still narrowband or poorly configured: - The company’s PBX/contact-center platform may convert the music to old **8 kHz telephone audio** before it ever reaches the carrier. - The source may be a low-bitrate MP3, an ancient looping audio file, or even audio fed from a cheap analog input. - Hold music is often intentionally processed with aggressive compression and level control so it remains audible on all phones. That makes music sound flat, harsh, and “tinny.” - Even if your handset and carrier support wideband calling, an interconnection between carriers—or the business’s call-center provider—may fall back to conventional narrowband telephony. - The hold state can use a different media server than the live call. So the agent’s voice may be reasonably clear while the music remains terrible. In short: HD Voice is possible end-to-end for a conversation, but hold music usually passes through legacy, low-priority infrastructure where nobody has much incentive to improve fidelity.
