14:51:42 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Exactly. It’s a long-standing feature of telephone culture, not a recent technical failure. For most of the 20th century, a phone line carried voice through a deliberately restricted audio channel. The standard “toll quality” band was roughly 300–3,400 Hz, because that was enough for understandable speech while conserving expensive network capacity. Music played into that channel lost its bass and treble immediately, producing the familiar tin-can sound your parents heard in the 1990s—and people were complaining about it well before then. What makes it persist is that hold music sits at the intersection of several things nobody prioritizes: - It is not the service the customer is paying for. - It is generally supplied by cheap PBX/contact-center hardware or a third-party music-on-hold vendor. - It has to work on every possible phone connection, including old landlines and poor mobile calls. - Businesses often care more about avoiding silence, licensing music, and inserting announcements than sound fidelity. - Replacing or reconfiguring old call-center audio systems has little visible payoff. So yes: it’s essentially a legacy telecom joke that survived because “good enough for speech” became “good enough for hold music,” and nobody had a compelling reason to spend money fixing it.
